Z||000002||Raydar||09-05-2001||07:35 PM||||Try sending an email to Ed Parks.<P>fierofactory@juno.com<P>If you ask him specific questions he can probably help you. <P>May take him a few days to answer, though. <BR>He stays busy.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||jelly2m8||09-05-2001||07:41 PM||||Best and easiest way is to get a V6 donor car, and do the work yourself. You need to swap the front compartment, interior, and rear lighting harness's as well as the engine harness. you need the V6 fuel lines, air filter assembly, etc, etc.<BR>On the 84 engine cradle, you need to drill the holes for the vibration dampner mount, or replace the cradle too. Guages get swapped, rad.. the list goes on. You will need to fabricate mounts to mount the air filter assembly, mount the power distrbution block either to the upper frame rail, or the strut tower. The electrical coolant fan in the trunk will require 3 holes to be cut, but most of my customers pass on adding that crap.<P> A customer just picked up a 85 GT thats severly rusted out for 300 dollars, now he has everything to swap a V6 in. If you can't find a cheap donor car, and have to pay someone to do the work, you are cheaper off selling the 84 and buyin a V6 car.<BR> I charge 1200 canuk in labor to change a 84 over to a V6. cheap donor cars usually run between 100-600 dollars. Then if you add in new tune up parts, belts,filters, the price goes up. Then usually with these cheap donor cars the engines need other work, like exhaust manifold gaskets, oil leaks fixed, sometimes water pumps, starters and alternators...<P>the price can add up real quickly sometimes. ||||reg||1
